## Common Dependency‑Injection Pitfalls in Spring (Java) – What to Watch For and How to Avoid Them

Below is a quick‑reference cheat sheet that covers the most frequent mistakes developers make when wiring beans in a Spring application, together with concrete patterns and best‑practice solutions.  
Feel free to cherry‑pick the items that are relevant to your codebase.

| # | Mistake | Why It Happens | Consequence | How to Fix / Avoid |
|---|---------|----------------|-------------|--------------------|
| 1 | **Field injection (`@Autowired` on a field)** | Quick and “magic” injection; many tutorials use it. | Hard to unit‑test, circular‑dependency detection is weak, no immutability guarantee, and IDEs warn about “unused field” because the compiler doesn’t see the setter. | Prefer **constructor injection**. It makes dependencies explicit, allows `final` fields, and works out‑of‑the‑box with Spring Boot testing. |
| 2 | **Setter injection (`@Autowired` on a setter)** | Legacy practice from early Spring; allows optional dependencies. | Can hide missing mandatory dependencies (a bean may start with a null dependency), circular dependencies can sneak in. | Use constructor injection for mandatory deps. Reserve setters for optional or configuration‑driven properties. |
| 3 | **Circular dependencies (A → B → A)** | Business logic ties two services together; forgetting that Spring can only break cycles for setter/property injection. | `UnsatisfiedDependencyException` or `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`. | Refactor: extract an interface or a **facade** bean, use **@Lazy** on one side, or redesign the domain so that one component only depends on the other’s interface. |
| 4 | **Injecting the wrong type or the wrong bean name** | Multiple beans of the same type, ambiguous injection, missing `@Qualifier`. | `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` or wrong bean is injected silently. | Use `@Qualifier` or `@Primary`, or rename beans with explicit names (`@Bean(name="myService")`). Prefer interfaces: `@Autowired MyService` + `@Component("myServiceImpl")`. |
| 5 | **Injecting `static` members or `static` methods** | Trying to get a global bean reference. | Static injection is not supported; the field stays `null`. | Never use static fields for Spring beans. If you need a global singleton, expose the bean via a static factory method in a configuration class, but the bean itself remains an instance. |
| 6 | **Using `new` inside a Spring‑managed bean** | Forgetting that Spring manages the bean lifecycle. | Dependencies inside the newly created object are not injected; you break the container’s control. | Let Spring create the bean (`@Component`, `@Bean`). For factories, use `@Bean` methods in a `@Configuration` class. |
| 7 | **Bean scopes mis‑used** (`@Scope("prototype")`, `"request"`, `"session"`) | Thinking that the default singleton scope will “just work”. | Thread‑safety bugs, accidental state leakage, `@RequestScope` beans in a non‑web context, or missing `@Scope("request")` leading to context‑not‑found errors. | Use `@Scope` only when needed. For web contexts, `@RequestScope` and `@SessionScope` are supported. Verify that a scoped bean is only injected into a **prototype** or **request** bean. |
| 8 | **Lazy initialization of beans (`@Lazy`)** | Attempting to avoid `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`. | `@Lazy` can mask true circular dependencies and cause subtle NPEs when the bean is accessed late. | Use `@Lazy` judiciously; prefer refactoring to remove cycles or use `ObjectProvider`/`Provider<T>` to resolve dependencies lazily. |
| 9 | **Mixing XML & Java config incorrectly** | Migrating an old project; forgetting to `@ComponentScan`. | `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`. | Keep a single source of truth: either pure Java config or XML. If you must use both, make sure the XML context imports the Java config (`<import>`) or vice‑versa. |
| 10 | **Mis‑configured `@Configuration` classes** | Declaring a `@Bean` method as `static` accidentally or forgetting `@ComponentScan`. | Static beans are not proxied; `@Autowired` on them fails; missing beans. | Use `@Bean` methods **non‑static** unless you truly need a pure utility bean. Make sure `@Configuration` classes are scanned. |
| 11 | **Using `@Autowired` on constructors of a class that isn’t a Spring bean** | Mixing Spring with plain Java objects. | No injection; the constructor runs without dependencies. | Make the class a Spring bean (`@Component`/`@Service`/`@Repository`) or inject it manually using `ApplicationContext#getBean(...)`. |
| 12 | **Using the wrong property placeholder syntax (`@Value("${…}")`)** | `application.properties` missing, wrong key, or placeholders not resolved. | `IllegalArgumentException: Could not resolve placeholder` at startup. | Use `@ConfigurationProperties` for complex configs. Verify that `@PropertySource` points to the correct file and that placeholders exist. |
| 13 | **Ignoring the lifecycle of beans (`@PostConstruct`, `@PreDestroy`)** | Not understanding that singleton beans live for the entire context. | Memory leaks, stale state. | Keep beans stateless when possible, or explicitly mark stateful beans with the right scope and clean them up in `@PreDestroy`. |
| 14 | **Overriding beans in tests (`@TestConfiguration`/`@MockBean`) incorrectly** | Test class uses `@Autowired` to pull a bean that gets overridden but still expects the real implementation. | Unexpected behavior, test flakiness. | In test config, use `@MockBean` or `@Bean` to provide the test implementation, and make sure that the test imports the right context (`@ContextConfiguration` or `@SpringBootTest(classes = ...)`). |
| 15 | **Not using `@EnableTransactionManagement` or missing `@Transactional`** | Assuming that JPA repositories handle transactions automatically. | Data inconsistency, transaction boundaries broken. | Add `@EnableTransactionManagement` (usually in Spring Boot it's auto‑enabled) and annotate service methods with `@Transactional` when you need transactional boundaries. |
| 16 | **Forgetting `@ComponentScan` or scanning wrong packages** | Project reorganized, but the `basePackages` still point to an old location. | Beans not registered; runtime failures. | Run `mvn dependency:tree` or use IDE to confirm the component scanning path. |
| 17 | **Multiple bean definitions with the same name** | Two `@Component` classes have `@Component("myService")` or two `@Bean(name="myService")` in separate `@Configuration` classes. | `BeanDefinitionStoreException: Failed to register bean definition` or ambiguous injection. | Ensure unique bean names or rely on the default naming (simple class name, camelCase). |
| 18 | **Using `@Primary` incorrectly** | Expecting the `@Primary` bean to win for all injection points. | In some places the non‑primary bean is still injected. | Use `@Primary` only when you truly want a default. Prefer `@Qualifier` for explicit choice. |
| 19 | **Injecting a collection of beans without ordering** | `List<MyService>` but you expect a particular order. | Order‑dependent logic fails. | Use `@Order` or `@Qualifier` on each bean, or sort the list manually. |
| 20 | **Using raw types (`List` instead of `List<MyInterface>`)** | Overlooking generics. | ClassCastException at runtime. | Always use generic types for injected collections. |

---

## Practical “Avoid‑it” Checklist

| # | Item | Quick Fix |
|---|------|-----------|
| 1 | **Prefer constructor injection** | `public MyService(DependencyA a, DependencyB b) { … }` |
| 2 | **Mark mandatory deps as `final`** | Ensures immutability. |
| 3 | **Use interfaces for component boundaries** | `public interface MyService { … }` + `@Service` implementation. |
| 4 | **Avoid `@Autowired` on fields** | IDE warns; use constructor injection. |
| 5 | **Inject by qualifier when multiple beans exist** | `@Autowired @Qualifier("myImpl") MyService service;` |
| 6 | **Keep your beans stateless** | If state is needed, use appropriate scopes (`@RequestScope`, `@SessionScope`). |
| 7 | **Don’t `new` inside a bean** | Let Spring create it; use `@Bean` or `@Component`. |
| 8 | **Use `@Lazy` only when truly needed** | Prefer refactoring over `@Lazy`. |
| 9 | **Make sure `@ComponentScan` covers all packages** | Add `basePackages = {"com.mycompany.app", "com.mycompany.services"}`. |
| 10 | **Test with `@SpringBootTest`** | It loads the full context and shows DI issues early. |
| 11 | **Use `@TestConfiguration` to override beans** | Keep production beans intact. |
| 12 | **Use `@ConfigurationProperties` for config** | Strongly typed config, validation, and documentation. |
| 13 | **Annotate service methods with `@Transactional`** | Guarantees ACID when needed. |
| 14 | **Avoid static injection** | Expose a bean factory if you need a static reference. |
| 15 | **Use `ObjectProvider<T>` or `Provider<T>` for lazy lookups** | Avoid circular deps and keep the container happy. |

---

## Example Refactor

**Before (common pitfall)**

```java
@Component
public class OrderProcessor {

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository repo;          // field injection

    @Autowired
    private PaymentService paymentService; // field injection

    @Autowired
    private NotificationService notificationService; // field injection

    public void process(Order order) {
        repo.save(order);
        paymentService.process(order);
        notificationService.send(order);
    }
}
```

**After (constructor‑only, immutable)**

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor  // Lombok – generates constructor
public class OrderProcessor {

    private final OrderRepository repo;
    private final PaymentService paymentService;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public void process(Order order) {
        repo.save(order);
        paymentService.process(order);
        notificationService.send(order);
    }
}
```

*Benefits:*

- **Compile‑time safety** – missing dependency triggers a compile error.
- **Testability** – you can pass mocks directly into the constructor.
- **Thread‑safety** – all fields are final.
- **No magic** – dependencies are explicit.

---

## Quick‑Start Testing Strategy

1. **Component‑level tests**  
   ```java
   @SpringBootTest
   @ActiveProfiles("test")
   class OrderProcessorTest {

       @Autowired OrderProcessor processor;
       @MockBean OrderRepository repo;
       @MockBean PaymentService paymentService;
       @MockBean NotificationService notificationService;

       @Test void shouldProcessOrder() {
           Order order = new Order(/*…*/);
           processor.process(order);
           verify(repo).save(order);
           verify(paymentService).process(order);
           verify(notificationService).send(order);
       }
   }
   ```

2. **Unit‑tests with plain JUnit** – inject mocks manually:  
   ```java
   @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
   class OrderProcessorUnitTest {

       @InjectMocks OrderProcessor processor;   // constructor injection
       @Mock OrderRepository repo;
       @Mock PaymentService paymentService;
       @Mock NotificationService notificationService;

       // test methods …
   }
   ```

*Why this helps:* You catch missing or miswired dependencies before the application starts, and you avoid false positives that only surface in integration tests.

---

## TL;DR – 5 “Golden Rules”

| # | Rule | One‑liner |
|---|------|-----------|
| 1 | **Constructor‑only DI** – no `@Autowired` fields. |
| 2 | **Mark mandatory deps `final`** – guarantees immutability. |
| 3 | **Prefer interfaces** – keep implementation details hidden. |
| 4 | **Scope correctly** – singleton for stateless beans, prototype/request/session only when required. |
| 5 | **Test early** – write unit tests with `@MockBean` / `@InjectMocks` to surface wiring errors before runtime. |

Follow these guidelines and the “Common mistakes” table, and you’ll have a clean, maintainable, and testable Spring DI layer that doesn’t bite you at production time. Happy coding!
